.TH BOSWAS-BOOTSPLASHD 8 "November 2026" "boswas-bootsplash 3.0~alpha1" "Boswas OS"
.SH NAME
boswas-bootsplashd \- the Boswas OS boot, disk-unlock and shutdown screen
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B /usr/libexec/boswas/boswas-bootsplashd
.RI [ OPTION ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B boswas-bootsplashd
shows the Boswas OS boot screen. While the system starts: the Boswas OS mark
on a dark background lit softly behind it in the brand's blues, and close
under it a progress ring, an arc that lengthens and shortens while it turns
(or fills with a reported percentage). At the start the mark assembles
itself: its stem rises, its bowl sweeps round and its dot lights up; then
the ring fades in. To unlock an encrypted disk the mark glides up and the
prompt slides in: the words "Unlock your disk", the name of the disk and a
text box that shows one dot for each character typed. While the system
shuts down or restarts: the mark, the ring and one line, "Shutting down" or
"Restarting". It is started from the initramfs and by systemd units, not by
hand.
.PP
It draws directly on the display through kernel mode setting (or a legacy
frame buffer), redraws only the part of the picture that changed, and reads
the passphrase from the console keyboard with the keymap the console uses.
Everything moves once per refresh of the display's mode, at most 240 times
a second (60 times on a legacy frame buffer, which does not tell its rate).
The mark and the text are drawn from vector outlines built into the
program, at the exact size of the display; it reads no image, font or theme
file at run time.
.PP
The boot screen is shown only when the kernel command line contains
.BR splash .
.B nosplash
(or
.BR boswas.splash=off )
turns it off;
.B boswas.splash=static
keeps the screen but stops the animation: the mark is shown whole at once,
the ring stands still, the caret of the text box does not blink, nothing
glides or fades, and the box does not shake when a passphrase is rejected.
When the boot screen does not run,
or cannot start, the disk passphrase is asked for on the text console as
usual.
.PP
The Escape key switches between the boot screen and the text console.
.PP
Only one boot screen runs at a time. Starting
.B boswas-bootsplashd
while one is running tells that one which screen to show
.RB ( \-\-mode )
and starts nothing; if the running one does not answer, it is left exactly
as it is.
.PP
Graphics devices change while a system starts: the firmware's frame buffer
is replaced by the driver of the graphics hardware when that driver loads.
The boot screen notices that its device is gone and continues on the new
one. If no display returns within two seconds it gives the screen to the
text console, where a passphrase prompt is then shown as a line of text,
and comes back when a display appears.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-\-mode= boot | shutdown | reboot
The screen to show (default:
.BR boot ).
.TP
.B \-\-static
Do not animate (see
.B boswas.splash=static
above).
.TP
.B \-\-force
Start even if the kernel command line does not ask for the boot screen.
.TP
.BI \-\-socket= PATH
Control socket (default:
.IR /run/boswas-bootsplash/socket ).
.TP
.BI \-\-tty= PATH
Console to read keys from (default: the virtual console in the foreground).
.TP
.BI \-\-wait= MILLISECONDS
How long to wait for a display to appear before giving up (default: 1500).
The daemon starts as soon as there is one.
.I boswas-bootsplash-start.service
passes 8000, because it can run before a graphics driver has found its
display. With
.B nomodeset
on the kernel command line no driver will load, and a longer wait is cut
to the default.
.TP
.BI \-\-headless= WIDTH x HEIGHT
Draw to memory instead of a display. Frames are saved on request in the
directory given with
.BR \-\-out .
For tests and review; needs no graphics device.
.TP
.BI \-\-out= DIRECTORY
Where headless frames are written.
.TP
.B \-\-foreground
Do not detach; run the boot screen in this process.
.TP
.B \-\-no-sandbox
Keep all privileges. For debugging only.
.TP
.B \-\-self-test
Enter the daemon's sandbox, report what it enforces, and exit: 0 when
everything is enforced, 3 when the kernel cannot enforce all of it (no
Landlock, or a Landlock without network rules before Linux 6.7) and the
rest is enforced, 1 otherwise.
.TP
.BR \-\-help ", " \-\-version
Print a summary of the options, or the version.
.SH SECURITY
The daemon runs as root because it must drive the display and read the
console before any user exists. Once the display, the console and the
control socket are open it keeps only what it needs: it sets
.BR no_new_privs ,
enters a Landlock domain that allows file access only to the graphics
devices, the console device and its runtime directory and denies TCP, and
drops all capabilities
except three (display master, console mode, following the root switch). It
has no network code. The passphrase is held in one buffer that is wiped
after use and is never written to a log. Clients must be root.
.PP
The Landlock rules name the graphics device directory as it is when the
daemon starts. A graphics device that appears later in a directory made
anew (the kernel removes and recreates
.I /dev/dri
when one driver replaces another) is opened by the supervising process and
passed to the daemon as an open descriptor. The supervising process is not
confined; it takes no input except that one request and passes nothing but
graphics device nodes.
.PP
The supervising process restores the text console and removes the control
socket and the pid file if the boot screen ends abnormally.
.PP
Neither process puts a console into text mode, or takes a display back,
once another program runs a graphical session there.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /run/boswas-bootsplash/socket
Control socket.
.TP
.I /run/boswas-bootsplash/pid
Process ID of the boot screen while it runs.
.TP
.I /run/boswas-bootsplash/lock
Locked for as long as a boot screen runs. The file stays when it ends.
.TP
.I /usr/share/initramfs-tools/hooks/boswas-bootsplash
Puts the boot screen and the graphics drivers into the initramfs.
.TP
.IR /usr/lib/systemd/system/emergency.service.d/50-boswas-bootsplash.conf ", " rescue.service.d/50-boswas-bootsplash.conf
End the boot screen before the emergency or rescue shell starts.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR boswas-bootsplash (1)
